## Pattern 2, share 07: near posts

### The parent asks for help with the emotional strain of caring for a child.

8 of 85 posts.

Parents ask for understanding, support or an explanation for distress while still feeding, soothing, playing with or taking their child out. Children are about two to 22 months, with one account involving a one- and three-year-old; the range includes depression, regret, suicidal thoughts, conflict with a partner and a retrospective account of improvement after relentless newborn crying.

- "I love my baby and i have never had any thought of hurting her in anyway! myself, yes! but I'm on medication and in therapy for this, but it's a long road and it isn't simple in the slightest, there's a lot of feelings and emotions and most importantly, hormones to battle with." [sr009-0016]
- "I want to just enjoy this and i have to admit, I am not a baby person! I thought i would be, but this shit is absolutely brutal! I am drowning every second of every day and having to just look like nothing is happening, I can sit in a room full of people with a smile on my face while absolutely feeling nothing inside of me. it's a horrific feeling and I would never wish it upon anyone!" [sr009-0014]
- "There have been so many days where all I just simply needed was a hug. Compassion and consideration with my husband right now in regard to me, is just completely thrown out the window, and I have no idea why. I’ve tried explaining to him how disregarded I’m feeling." [sr020-0003]
- "I feel bad for my son because I know I’m not as involved as I can be. I feel depressed when I come home and I don’t play with him or really interact with him unless it’s to feed or change him or do the essentials." [sr063-0031]
- "[…] I love my baby so much and I’m always trying to be the best version of myself around her, always cheerful, always doing little activities in the house for her development." [sr070-0073]
- "[…] I also feel so selfish because I just want 1 or 2 hours to myself to do SOMETHING I enjoy, like read or color. And I feel selfish for even thinking that because I have a baby and they need my attention, and not their fault 🥺" [sr102-0078]
- "[…] If I don’t have a plan for every day I just don’t know how I’ll cope. I don’t know what to do all day alone with him when he’s just so on the go. He’s too little for anything to really hold his attention for very long. My partner works 10 hour days and it feels like forever." [sr107-0057]
- "[…] I spent weeks regretting my choice to have a baby and wishing I could have my old life back. I’m thankful we’re coming out of the trenches but honestly mourning what I had thought my newborn life would be, cuddling and ogling him, passing him off to family to snuggle with. […]" [sr130-0085]

**But:** The parent initially judging a partner’s care later acknowledges not knowing the full picture; that account is second-hand about the wife’s experience. "Y’all are right I probably don’t have the full picture of what she goes through and how difficult it is for her compared to me, both with the kids just being tougher with her and also her having to do it every day." [sr022-0161]

