### The parent wants the day to be enjoyable for themselves as well as the child.

9 of 85 posts.

Parents describe boredom during play, lost hobbies, a changed identity or feeling trapped in repeated care, and ask how to enjoy the day or whether work would suit them better. Explicit ages range from a four-and-a-half-month-old to two-year-olds, with some ages unknown; love, gratitude and support can coexist with these feelings.

- "Thanks for asking. Even though I enjoy plenty of small moments throughout the day, in general, the days feel boring and repetitive and a lot of hard work. So I end up looking forward to a break, whether that’s nap time, when my spouse gets home from work, when the babysitter comes, or when grandma visits… But then when I have this time off, I’m so tired and burnt out that I end up not doing anything fun or restorative. I think I also struggle a bit with how intense it is right now. […]" [sr012-0094]
- "My SIL is amazing and has stayed home with all 4 of her kids. She does a truly incredible job of “entering into their world” constantly during the day and is so great at meeting them where they are, being playful, slowing down to allow them to learn and explore, etc. I am so jealous of how easily she does this and how she seems to enjoy it so much because I do not. […]" [sr018-0182]
- "I like to colour but I can’t colour. I like to paint but can’t paint. I like to make bracelets, I can’t do that. I put a show on and read, or go on my phone.those are the things I can do and easily involve her, or do while she contact naps. I have been told to include her in the painting and colouring but we do. She has her own painting and colouring time sometimes if I feel like cleaning up the mess, we do that together. […]" [sr022-0027]
- "But playing with my toddler often really bores me. We'll be sitting together, me doing something, him doing something else and at some point I just zone out and pick up my phone. When he is playing by himself I am actually relieved because I get some time to myself and immediately reach for my phone. When he then tries to engage me, it takes me a while to react." [sr057-0019]
- "I feel like everything has gotten worse..since becoming a mom. My physical health, my mental health, even my job performance...cuz I'm always tired. It's this all encompassing thing..that takes over your life and it feels like I don't exist anymore..like I only exist to take care of this little human. And that..leaves me feeling empty..like a shell of myself. The days tend to be repetitive and kind of boring, but exhausting at the same time. […]" [sr072-0038]
- "I genuinely feel like a slave. I’m completely exhausted. […] We wanted this child, so I know I shouldn’t complain, but honestly, I never imagined it would be this hard." [sr097-0015]

**But:** One parent explicitly enjoys slow days, attachment and autonomy, while still feeling lost about identity and work. "Honestly, I do genuinely feel so grateful for this life. For many reasons, I get to bond with my daughter and develop a healthy attachment with her, I get to live life at our own pace, I get to enjoy coffees slowly and I get to live life on our own terms. (Our, because my daughter is the one who is really in charge!)" [sr118-0036]

